{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:f19afca16401a812803b5773d32120e8b12c6450ca2f0753e92717234982e051","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.3,"B":0.45,"C":0.15,"D":0.1}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED EXPLICITLY, in lexical-priority order, against my own CQ3 and CQ2. INFLATION MARGINAL FOR THE 2032-36 SUB-WINDOW: I side with P3 against P2 on the PROFILE. P2 argues a RISING profile (so 2032-36 runs hotter than the 2027-36 mean) because the care-Baumol, scarcity and interest-claim terms strengthen monotonically. P3 argues FRONT-LOADED, because OECD core is observed at 3.6% now and energy inflation above 10% now, which near-locks 2027-29. P3 is right and P2's framing is narrative coherence mistaken for likelihood: a rising-profile story is tidier, but the observed starting level is a fact and the monotone-strengthening terms are a model. Given my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal of 0.68 on a 2027-36 mean of ~2.6% with 2027-29 at ~2.8%, the 2032-36 sub-window central is ~2.40% with sigma 0.50 (sigma widened for the AI-Q4 digestion branch). P(core >= 2.7) = 0.27; P(core < 2.0) = 0.21. GROWTH MARGINAL from my own CQ3 (P10 1.0 / P50 2.1 / P90 3.3; sigma_up 0.94, sigma_down 0.86): P(g >= 2.6) = 0.30, P(g < 2.0) = 0.45, P(g < 1.7) = 0.32. STEP 1: A = P(g >= 2.6) = 0.30 -> A = 30. The lock makes rule 1 a pure growth test, so I do NOT haircut for A's qualitative clauses (debt stabilisation, broad wage gains); as P1 correctly notes, applying them would lower A, so 30 is an upper bound on the substantive regime. 30 is a very large deviation from the Wave-1 centre of 11.3, and it is ENTIRELY inherited from my CQ3 — it is not an independent judgment about an AI renaissance. If my attack on the China double-subtraction fails, A collapses to ~17 and B absorbs it. STEP 2: C = P(core >= 2.7 AND g < 2.0). 0.45 x 0.33 = 0.149 -> C = 15, where I raise the conditional from 0.27 to 0.33 for the supply-wall co-occurrence P3 documents (grid/energy inflation already >10%, electrician deficit ~107k peaking 2027, care vacancies with Baumol and a strained administered payer). C is DERIVED from my CQ2, not asserted: my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal of 0.68 applies to >=2.50% on the 2027-36 mean, and C needs the tighter >=2.70% on the later, COOLER sub-window AND simultaneous sub-2.0% growth, so C must sit well below 0.68 — it does, at 0.15. STEP 3: D = P(g < 1.7 AND core < 2.0), disjoint from C by construction. 0.32 x 0.32 = 0.103 -> D = 10, conditional raised from 0.21 to 0.32 for the capex-bust/BIS-demand-deficit cell. STEP 4: B = residual = 100 - 30 - 15 - 10 = 45. SUM = 100 exactly. CROSS-CHECK ON THE DIRECTION OF MY DEVIATION: relative to Wave 1 I move ~19 points from C+D (Wave-1 centre 29.3, mine 25) and from B (Wave-1 centre 49.3, mine 45) into A. That is the correct accounting of my two attacks: the China double-subtraction and the Baumol double-count both move growth mass up without moving inflation mass, so A rises and the two low-growth cells shrink while B — the residual — barely moves. A RED-TEAM FINDING ON THE TAXONOMY ITSELF, extending P2's: P2 flags that his B mass sits at 1.5% growth, BELOW B's own stated 1.7-2.5% band. At my P50 of 2.1% the label and my central case coincide, which means my B is the only one in this effort that means what it says — and that is a warning about the aggregation, not a virtue of mine: three of four payloads in this effort put their modal mass in a cell whose label misdescribes their own central growth rate by ~0.5 pp, so a reader averaging the CQ6 vectors across the effort will recover a B that no panelist actually holds.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:5